                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 237. From the. Times, Friday
                        September 1,1882.]</seg></opener>
                <p>The press association Ennis correspondent says that Mr. Stephen J. Meany,
                    correspondent of the New York Star, who was arrested on the 10th of August under
                    the crime prevention act, and bound over in his own recognizances, attended
                    yesterday at the police barracks in accordance with instructions from the
                    American legation in London to surrender to his bail. The subinspector, however,
                    declined to take any action and referred him to Mr. Purcell, stipendiary
                    magistrate. Mr. Meany, acting on advice from the American Government, is
                    determined to test the legality of his arrest, and has adopted this mode of
                    bringing the question to a decisive issue.</p>
            </div>
